                    "text": "The image shows Мысық, a character a child drew. Personality: Curious and brave; never scared of cups. Ability: jump. Its lines, by index: 0: \"Hi!\", 1: \"Let's go!\", 2: \"I'm here!\", 3: \"Look!\", 4: \"Well done!\". In an AR game the character is a flat picture standing on a table, and you move the whole picture. Write five short emotes for it: hello (a greeting), bye (a goodbye, a bow or a wave), dance (bounces to a beat), spin (turns around at least once), roar (puffs up and shakes). Each emote is {\"durationMs\": 300 to 4000, \"keys\": 2 to 16 poses sorted by atMs, the first at atMs 0 and the last at atMs durationMs, \"line\": the index 0 to 4 of the line it says when it starts, \"sound\": one of \"none\",\"hello\",\"roar\",\"dance\"}. A pose is {\"atMs\": ms from the start, \"scaleX\": 0.6 to 1.5 width, \"scaleY\": 0.6 to 1.5 height, \"yawDeg\": turn about the vertical axis in degrees, counted up (360 is one full turn, at most 360 between two poses), \"tiltDeg\": -35 to 35 lean sideways (a wave, a bow), \"hop\": 0 to 0.5 lift in character heights}. The rest pose is scaleX 1, scaleY 1, yawDeg 0, tiltDeg 0, hop 0; the last pose is the rest pose with yawDeg a whole number of turns, so the picture faces the camera again. Between poses the picture moves smoothly. Fit the moves to the personality: a shy character waves small, a wild one roars big. Reply with JSON only: {\"hello\": emote, \"bye\": emote, \"dance\": emote, \"spin\": emote, \"roar\": emote}."
                },
                {
                    "type": "image_url",
                    "image_url": {
                        "url": "data:{{INPUT_MIME}};base64,{{INPUT_B64}}"
                    }
                }
            ]
        }
